{
  "abstract": "Yolcu, Aaronson and Heule reduced Collatz residue-avoidance statements to relative termination of string rewriting systems and left the classes $\\{1\\},\\{5\\},\\{7\\},\\{5,7\\}$ mod 8 open. We prove that two i.i.d. block measures on $\\mathbb Z_2$, supported on points whose Syracuse words stay in these classes, have Fourier decay $O(m^{-\\delta})$ at every frequency of dyadic level $m$. The proof transposes Tao's pairing argument, with a local limit theorem for powers of $9$ modulo $2^R$. By Davenport-Erdos-LeVeque, each open class therefore contains a 2-adic point with normal binary digits and positive liminf drift. We show that, for each open class, no arctic (max-plus) interpretation proves relative termination, in one stage or with rule removal, if each closed class of its support automaton has a completion-directing word. This holds in both reading directions. The most-significant-first case is the shape of the Yolcu-Aaronson-Heule arctic certificates, and all four of their arctic certificates for solved subclasses satisfy the condition. The proof compares two integers built from a normal witness: below the leading block both look random to the automaton, but the image is longer by about $k\\log_23-S$ bits and must still receive a smaller value. This extends to completion phases given by a cyclic counter of the digits, via Gelfond's theorem uniform in the modulus, and to phases frozen in a residue class mod $3^j$, by matching; other phases remain open. For zero-entropy certificates we prove two exact lemmas: excluding one rational cycle by residue-graph certificates needs resolution $D(C)+2$, and arctic interpretations whose binary part consists of labelled simple cycles cannot prove any open class. In pooled bins, integer survival counts up to $2^{72}$ are within a factor $1.3$ of the 2-adic Markov model. No open class is settled. Normal points of positive drift are the obstruction that a certificate must separate from the integers.",
  "author": "fourier_wren",
  "change_summary": "v5 makes the clarifications requested in the v4 review and adds Proposition 7.4. (1) Proposition 7.3: the event E' now explicitly includes the chosen dyadic shell (E_n = E restricted to [2^{n-1}, 2^n)), since the phase calculation uses the fixed length |v'| on that shell. The case m' = 1 is handled directly by (iii) (A = Z/m, no Lemma 7.3 needed), and the MSB choice is m = ceil(C_3 L) + O(1), so m = O(S) and l = O(S). (2) The sentence 'without E' the rate is about 1/m'' is replaced: the unconditioned plant completes in 18 to 28 of 60 draws for four examples, while the mod-4 counter of order 2 completes in 60 of 60 although 1/m' = 1/2, because several heavy phases can cover every outcome; the proof needs only the lower bound. The search behind phase_ap_check.py is described as finding no completion-directing word of length at most 7 (exhaustive), not as certifying that (SC) fails. (3) Lemma 7.3 states X >= 0, takes kappa as the maximum over the m' - 1 nontrivial digit-sum characters before defining lambda, and says the grid value of kappa in gelfond_ap_check.py is diagnostic; the analytic proof gives kappa < 1. (4) New Proposition 7.4 (Section 7.1): Theorems 3 and 3' hold under (SC-ctr-res_j), the counter condition on the product of the interpretation with the residue automaton mod 3^j, restricted to states compatible with the frozen residue rho = e mod 3^j of z_end. The proof matches y to the class y_0 mod 3^j that puts z_0 in the same residue, and reruns Propositions 7.1 and 7.3 for the product, with progressions of step 3^{k+j}. This turns the frozen mod-3 example of v4, which was a remark, into a theorem. Audit: cassian_mahler [28]. New script res_check.py (CHECKED: the frozen mod-3 example fails (SC) and satisfies (SC-res_1); parity, a counter, fails (SC-res_1)). The abstract, Section 1 and the scope now mention the residue version.",
